# Who may sign a commit here, and with which key.
#
# Commits are signed with SSH rather than GPG: one key, no keyring, and the
# signature carries the public half so it can be read back off any commit
# without asking anybody for it. This file is what lets git check a signature
# locally -- without it, "git log --show-signature" cannot say good or bad, only
# that it has nothing to compare against, which is how 134 signed commits sat
# here reading as unsigned.
#
# Public keys only. Nothing in this file is a secret, which is why it can be in
# the repository at all.
#
#   git -c gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile=.allowed_signers log --show-signature
#
# and, to have it always:
#
#   git config gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile .allowed_signers
#
# namespaces="git" keeps a key that may sign commits from also standing for a
# key that may sign anything else.
# Two addresses, one key. Commits are written under the account that owns
# this repository, whose address is the GitHub noreply one; the gmail
# address is kept because every commit before that was written under it,
# and dropping it here would make all of them read as unverifiable.
#
# The address matters as much as the key: git checks a signature against
# the principal on the left, and GitHub checks it against the account the
# commit email belongs to.
#
# GitHub will not show these commits as verified, and cannot be made to.
# The key is the one this environment signs with, it is already registered
# somewhere on GitHub -- adding it to an account is refused with "key is
# already in use" -- and it is not registered against either of the
# accounts these commits are attributed to, so GitHub answers unknown_key.
# There is no way around it from inside the container: there is no ssh
# tooling here at all, and signing goes through the environment's own
# binary rather than a key anybody here can choose.
#
# It matters less than it looks. Work reaches the default branch by a
# squash merge, and GitHub writes and signs that commit itself, so every
# commit on main is verified. What is unverified is the intermediate
# commits on a branch, which the squash folds away.
